[0mOtto: Done. Each skill gets its own folder, skills/<name>/SKILL.md. The file starts with a "---" block holding the skill's name and one line on when to use it, which is the same layout Anthropic's Agent Skills use. When I start, agent.ts reads only those lines and adds one line per skill to the end of my system message, after otto.md and AGENTS.md. The full instructions stay in the file until a task needs them, and then I read them with read_file, so I didn't need a new tool. I also added a paragraph to otto.md telling me to work that way and how to lay out new skills.

My first skill is add-a-tool. It covers the two parts of a tool: its description in request.tools and its code in runTool. It also explains why the description matters (the PowerShell mix-up is in there) and lists the AGENTS.md steps for changing myself.

